course: dictionary
lesson: 11116
title: A tiny word with a big meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A whit is a very tiny amount. You almost always meet it in a negative phrase, such as «not a whit» or «no whit». «He cared not a whit what the neighbours thought.» The word not is the clue: he did not care at all, not even a tiny bit.
explain: Look at the words around whit, which is its context. In «The rain did not bother her a whit», the sentence tells you the rain did not bother her even slightly. Try swapping in "not at all" for «not a whit». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
explain: Whit can also be short for Whitsun or Whit Sunday, a church feast in the UK, and it starts with a capital letter. «Whit Monday» was once a holiday. Ask yourself: is this a tiny amount, or a day in the church year? The clues will tell you.
explain: Be careful with Scots. In Scotland, «whit» is a different word that means what. «Whit's the magic word, son?» means What is the magic word? Scots is closely related to English, so some words look the same but mean different things. A dictionary settles it.
fact: A whit is a very tiny amount, like a speck.
fact: «Not a whit» means not at all, and «no whit» means the same.
fact: «Every whit» means every single bit, as in «every whit of it».
fact: Whit is an old-fashioned word, found in classic stories, the Bible and old poems.
fact: Whit Sunday, or Whitsun, is a church feast called Pentecost, when Christians believe the Holy Spirit came to Jesus's followers.
exercise: «He cared not a whit what the neighbours thought.» What does «not a whit» mean? | A kind of hat | *Not at all | A very large amount | A loud noise
exercise: Whit Sunday is also called Whitsun. Which church feast is it? | A harvest supper | A summer fair | *Pentecost | A school sports day
exercise: «The rain did not bother her a whit.» What does «a whit» tell you? | A large crowd | A fast car | A bird that sings at dawn | *A tiny amount
test: In Glasgow, a dad asks, «Whit's the magic word, son?» What does whit mean here? | Where | Who | *What | Why
test: Which phrase uses «whit» to mean a tiny amount? | «Whitsun» is a church feast | *«Not a whit» means not at all | «Whit's the magic word?» in Scotland | «Whit Monday was a holiday.»
exam: «She did not care a whit about the prize.» What does this mean? | Only once | Very much | Quite a lot | *Not at all
